/-- Scalar realization of compact support and its Frobenius invariant counit.
Source: latest blueprint, sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package,
cor-coef-verdier-weil-equivariance.proof.md, final paragraph
(blueprint: lem-sf-native-weil-scalar-frobenius-counit).

Proof plan, retaining all seventeen DAG steps:
1. The finite-span and finite-subextension coefficient sheaves agree: a finite-dimensional
$\mathbf Q_\ell$-span of algebraic elements lies in the field generated by a basis, a finite
extension; the converse is immediate. Use [lem-sf-finite-span-module-derived-comparison] for the
resulting coefficient isomorphism and its precomposition actions. It identifies the internal-
module and sectionwise-module proper derived images, including their adjunctions. The open
comparison is [lem-sfb-internal-module-operations-transport]: both open extensions are the
sheafification of the same extension-by-zero presheaf. Exactness of that extension [lem-pur-site-
restriction-module-adjunction-exact] means that applying it termwise is its derived functor.
Therefore the $P_\Omega^c=Rp_{*,\Omega}j_{!,\Omega}$ occurring in the condensed realization is
exactly the functor $F^c$ in the assertion under these specified comparisons, also on morphisms.
Uses: lem-sf-finite-span-module-derived-comparison, lem-sfb-internal-module-operations-transport,
lem-pur-site-restriction-module-adjunction-exact.

2. For a finite-stage representative $(E,K_E)$ choose its integral object representative $K$, with
valuation ring $R$, uniformizer $t$ and reductions $A_n=R/t^n$. By [lem-weil-compact-support-
reduction-tower-functor] the actual strict tower $\mathcal T_f^c(K)$ has the levels
$\operatorname{Res}_nRp_{*,n}j_!K_n$ and the quotient/restriction transition maps specified in
[def-weil-integral-rational-qbar-compact-support]. Thus the integral $F_fK$ is exactly
$P_RK=R\lim\mathcal T_f^c(K)$, and $F_f(E,K_E)=(E,(P_RK)[1/t])$. The identification is of the
strict diagram and its functor, not an inference from equal derived evaluations. Uses: lem-weil-
compact-support-reduction-tower-functor, def-weil-integral-rational-qbar-compact-support.

3. We check the domain needed for the factor-normalization statement below. The integral
representative $K$ of Step 2 is an object of $D^b_c(X,\mathcal O_E)$ of [def-weil-derived-
complete-integral-detailed]; for a separated finite-type $k$-scheme these are exactly the objects
of the complete integral category $\mathcal I_X(R)$ of [def-weil-pbc-bounded-constructible-
complete], with the same completed coefficient sheaf and reductions [lem-pur-rational-lattice-
adic-domain], so $K$ is an admissible input of the realization and normalization lemmas. $X,Z,Y$
are separated finite-type $k$-schemes, where $Z$ is the chosen compactification. They are
noetherian by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian] applied over $\operatorname{Spec}k$ (a
field has only the ideals $0,k$), and have finite dimension by [lem-kun-dimension-finite-type].
Apply [lem-cbc-integral-compact-support-uniform-restriction] first to the open $j$ with
compactification $(j,1_Z)$ and then to proper $p$ with $(1_Z,p)$. It puts $j_{!,B}K$ and
$P_R^0K=Rp_{*,B}j_{!,B}K$ in the complete uniform integral categories, including their compatible
etale-origin witnesses. $P_RK$ belongs to the same category by [lem-pur-compact-support-condensed-
realization]. Apply [lem-pur-integral-reduction-uniform-tor] on each of $X,Z,Y$ to the finite list
of these objects occurring there. Taking the minimum of their lower bounds and maximum of their
upper bounds gives common finite cohomological and Tor bounds. Reindexing carries their local
models and witnesses to the same relabeled models. These verify all input and intermediate
hypotheses of the normalization lemma; they are not an assumption that ordinary ringed proper
image equals strict-tower proper image. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian, lem-kun-
dimension-finite-type, lem-cbc-integral-compact-support-uniform-restriction, lem-pur-compact-
support-condensed-realization, lem-pur-integral-reduction-uniform-tor, lem-pur-rational-lattice-
adic-domain, def-weil-derived-complete-integral-detailed, def-weil-pbc-bounded-constructible-
complete.

4. Use [lem-zl-compact-support-realization-factor-normalization], whose hypotheses were checked in
Step 3. Write $r_W\dashv L_W$ for reduction and total limit, $V=S_pJ_j$ for the semilinear-system
proper/open functor, and $u$ for the reduction/limit unit. It specifies a system map
$b_K:r_Y(P_R^0K)\to V(r_XK)$, obtained from the proper coefficient counit mate followed by the
unit-normalized open scalar map, and fixes
$$q_K=L_Y(b_K)u_{P_R^0K}:P_R^0K\xrightarrow{\sim}P_RK.$$ With $J_W$ the continuous $E$-coefficient
realization, its exact factor formulas are
$$a_{f,E,K}=Rp_{*,E}(\delta_j^J)\,\chi_p^J\,J_Y(q_K^{-1}[1/t]),\qquad
\xi_{f,E,D}=Rp_{*,D}(\delta_j^{E,D})\,\pi_p^{E,D}.$$ Here $D=\Omega$ or a finite extension,
$\chi_p^J$ is the counit-compatible direct-image comparison of $J$, and $\pi_p^{E,D}$ is the
transpose of the specified pullback/tensor balancing followed by the extended proper counit. These
are the actual specified comparisons, including the integral coordinate change $q_K^{-1}$. Uses:
lem-zl-compact-support-realization-factor-normalization.

5. Put $T_W=\Omega_W^c\otimes^L_{E_W^c}-$ and identify $\rho_W(E,K_E)=T_WJ_WK_E$ using [lem-sf-
adic-qbar-constructible-realization]. Define the finite-stage component
$$\alpha_{(E,K_E)}=\xi_{f,E,\Omega,J_XK_E}\,T_Y(a_{f,E,K}).$$ Its factors are isomorphisms by
[lem-pur-compact-support-condensed-realization]. That statement also supplies naturality on
rational morphisms, the mixed finite-extension compatibility with $a$, and identity, extension-
tower and shift coherences. For a common-stage arrow choose a common finite field $H$ as in [def-
weil-algebraically-closed-coefficients]; over $H$ the naturality square for this composite
commutes. A different representative becomes the same arrow over a further common field, so the
mixed square makes the components and their naturality independent of the representative. Balanced
tensor multiplication is exactly the scalar comparison of $\rho$. Consequently the identity,
extension and shift equations descend as equalities of natural transformations. This constructs
$\alpha:\rho_YF_f\simeq F^c\rho_X$, using the functor identifications of Steps 1--2. Uses: lem-sf-
adic-qbar-constructible-realization, lem-pur-compact-support-condensed-realization, def-weil-
algebraically-closed-coefficients.

6. Fix the Frobenius square of the chosen $k_0$-compactification. Its three geometric Frobenii are
scheme isomorphisms and the proper and open squares commute and are Cartesian [lem-weil-frobenius-
square-cartesian]. Write $H_W=g_W^{-1}$ on completed modules and $H_W^\bullet$ on coefficient
systems. The specified completed pullbacks identify with $H_W$ by [lem-pur-isomorphism-completed-
reduction-reindexing]. Use the comparisons $$t_W:r_WH_W\xrightarrow{\sim}H_W^\bullet r_W,\qquad
\lambda_W:H_WL_W\xrightarrow{\sim}L_WH_W^\bullet.$$ By [lem-pod-reindexing-completion-unit-tensor]
they preserve the actual reduction/limit units and counits, quotient arrows and total-limit
projections. In particular $$L_W(t_{W,M})u_{H_WM}=\lambda_{W,r_WM}H_W(u_M).$$ All maps in this
equation are whole-system comparisons before taking any evaluated reduction. Uses: lem-weil-
frobenius-square-cartesian, lem-pur-isomorphism-completed-reduction-reindexing, lem-pod-
reindexing-completion-unit-tensor.

7. We also need reindexing of the finite-field realization $J$, separately from $\rho$. Its
definition in [lem-weil-pbc-condensed-e-realization] is derived coefficient extension
$E_W^c\otimes^L_{B_W}-$ after rationalization. On a flat-term K-flat representative, reindexing
preserves this tensor: the coefficient identifications carry a relabeled $x\otimes c$ to the same
$x\otimes c$. [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-tensor-pullback] supplies the canonical derived
balancing and preservation of such representatives. Together with the specified completed pullback
identification in Step 6 this gives $J_WH_W\simeq H_{W,E}J_W$ on raw rationalizations, even though
$g_W$ is not $k$-linear. The same calculation gives $T_WH_{W,E}\simeq H_{W,\Omega}T_W$. For a
composite coefficient/pullback square both routes send $(x\otimes c)\otimes d$ to the relabeled
$x\otimes cd$; associativity and the coefficient unit give identity and composition equations.
Their composite for $T_WJ_W$ is exactly $b_{g_W}$ of [lem-sf-adic-realization-isomorphism-
normalization], which fixes the same quotient cocones and balanced tensor maps. This verifies the
left-hand faces of the mate cubes at $E$ and $\Omega$, rather than obtaining the $E$ comparison
from an assertion only about $\rho$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-condensed-e-realization, lem-weil-pbc-
ringed-topoi-derived-tensor-pullback, lem-sf-adic-realization-isomorphism-normalization.

8. The proper coefficient comparisons commute with these reindexings by taking their specified
transposes. For a coefficient extension $S_A^D$, the proper map $S_YRp_{*,A}\to Rp_{*,D}S_Z$ is
adjunct to $S_Z(\varepsilon_{p,A})\mu$, with $\mu$ the pullback/tensor balancing [lem-pur-ringed-
coefficient-projection-cocycle]. Reindexing that transpose and using Step 7 gives the identical
balanced pullback map followed by the reindexed counit. The geometric square comparison itself has
transpose the reindexed proper counit: at completed coefficients and on towers this is [lem-pur-
isomorphism-completed-reduction-reindexing]. At the other coefficient rings it follows directly
from the site equivalence of [lem-weil-proetale-isomorphism-inverse-image]: both underived direct
images evaluate a sheaf on $Z\times_YV$, with the same coefficient action; the exact equivalence
and its inverse preserve injectives, so the identity derives with its counit. Pasting these faces
and applying the mate bijection gives equality of the two proper coefficient routes [lem-weil-pbc-
mate-pasting]. The same argument applies to $\chi_p^J$, since [lem-weil-pbc-condensed-e-
realization] identifies its actual adjunction counits and its pullback comparison is the balanced
one in Step 7. It also applies to $\pi_p^{E,\Omega}$ by Step 4's transpose formula. No
identification with the different common-stage comparison $b_p:\rho_YRp_*\to Rp_{*,B}\rho_Z$ is
used. Uses: lem-pur-ringed-coefficient-projection-cocycle, lem-pur-isomorphism-completed-
reduction-reindexing, lem-weil-proetale-isomorphism-inverse-image, lem-weil-pbc-mate-pasting, lem-
weil-pbc-condensed-e-realization.

9. For open extension, [lem-sf-open-tensor-normalization] characterizes the inverse of each scalar
or geometric open comparison as the open-adjunction transpose of the open unit. Under the
coefficient and reindexing comparisons of Step 7, restricting either route to the open gives the
identity on the same balanced tensor. Thus their adjuncts coincide, proving the open
coefficient/reindexing squares for $\delta_j^J$ and $\delta_j^{E,D}$. The statement applies also
to ringed diagram sites with arbitrary strict coefficient transitions. Applying the same reasoning
there proves the open-system square. This argument uses the unit normalization, not merely
existence of an open comparison isomorphism. Uses: lem-sf-open-tensor-normalization.

10. Apply Steps 8--9 to the ring-system map $\Delta B\to(A_n)_n$ defining $b_K$. The simultaneous
operations and adjunctions exist on the derived category of systems by [lem-coef-semilinear-
derived-sections]; the coefficient projection identities of [lem-pur-ringed-coefficient-
projection-cocycle] hold there before evaluation. Write $c^0:H_YP_R^0\simeq P_R^0H_X$ for the
ordinary completed proper/open reindexing, and $e:H_Y^\bullet V\simeq VH_X^\bullet$ for the system
mate followed by open exchange. The factor definition of $b_K$ in [lem-zl-compact-support-
realization-factor-normalization] and the two commuting factor squares give the typed system
equation $$e_{r_XK}\,H_Y^\bullet(b_K)\,t_{Y,P_R^0K} =V(t_{X,K})\,b_{H_XK}\,r_Y(c^0_K).$$ Its
source is $r_YH_YP_R^0K$ and target $VH_X^\bullet r_XK$. This is obtained by pasting actual
derived-system mate transformations, rather than deduced from their individual evaluations. Uses:
lem-coef-semilinear-derived-sections, lem-pur-ringed-coefficient-projection-cocycle, lem-zl-
compact-support-realization-factor-normalization.

11. Now retain the $q_K$ factor. Under $P_R=L_YVr_X$ set
$$c^R_K=(L_YV(t_{X,K}))^{-1}L_Y(e_{r_XK})\lambda_{Y,Vr_XK}:H_YP_RK\to P_RH_XK.$$ This is the
specified integral compact-support Frobenius exchange: [def-weil-frobenius-square-exchange]
defines it by these operation comparisons and the whole system map $e$, and [lem-weil-frobenius-
system-exchange-inverse] gives its inverse as the total limit of the inverse system exchange with
the inverse operation comparisons. Using $q_K=L_Y(b_K)u$, naturality of $\lambda$, the unit
identity of Step 6 and the equation of Step 10 yields $$\begin{aligned} c^R_KH_Y(q_K)
&=(L_YVt_X)^{-1}L_Y(e\,H_Y^\bullet b_K)\lambda H_Y(u_{P_R^0K})\\
&=(L_YVt_X)^{-1}L_Y(e\,H_Y^\bullet b_K\,t_Y)u_{H_YP_R^0K}\\
&=L_Y(b_{H_XK})L_Yr_Y(c^0_K)u_{H_YP_R^0K}\\ &=q_{H_XK}c^0_K. \end{aligned}$$ The last equality is
naturality of the actual unit of $r_Y\dashv L_Y$ [lem-coef-derived-system-evaluation-calculus].
Thus $q$ and $q^{-1}$ intertwine the specified strict-tower exchange and the ordinary completed
proper/open comparison. Rationalizing this identity is legitimate on the integral representatives.
No equality of integral morphisms is inferred from equality of reductions. Uses: def-weil-
frobenius-square-exchange, lem-weil-frobenius-system-exchange-inverse, lem-coef-derived-system-
evaluation-calculus.

12. Paste Step 11 with the proper and open squares of Steps 8--9 in the exact factor formulas of
Step 4. This proves reindexing compatibility of $a_{f,E,K}$ and $\xi_{f,E,\Omega}$, hence of their
composite $\alpha$. To spell out its type, let $P_s,Q_s$ be common-stage Frobenius pullbacks, and
$e^s:Q_sF_f\simeq F_fP_s$ be the specified exchange of [def-weil-frobenius-square-exchange]. Its
integral representative is exactly the exchange in Step 11, and its common-stage image is the
ordered reindexing exchange by [lem-pur-weil-semilinear-exchange-reindexing]. Put $d_X=
b_{g_X}:\rho_XP_s\simeq P\rho_X$ and $d_Y=b_{g_Y}:\rho_YQ_s\simeq Q\rho_Y$ from [lem-sf-adic-
realization-isomorphism-normalization], and let $a^c:QF^c\simeq F^cP$ be the actual module
proper/open comparison. The pasted equality is $$a^c_{\rho_XK}\,Q(\alpha_K)\,d_{Y,F_fK}
=F^c(d_{X,K})\,\alpha_{P_sK}\,\rho_Y(e^s_K).$$ Both sides go from $\rho_YQ_sF_fK$ to
$F^cP\rho_XK$. Step 5 descends this equation on every common-stage arrow. This explicitly compares
both specified exchanges, not just the objects they act on. Uses: def-weil-frobenius-square-
exchange, lem-pur-weil-semilinear-exchange-reindexing, lem-sf-adic-realization-isomorphism-
normalization.

13. By [lem-sf-adic-qbar-constructible-realization], every $M\in\mathcal C_X$ is isomorphic to
some $\rho_XK$. Then $F^cM\simeq F^c\rho_XK\simeq\rho_YF_fK$ is in $\mathcal C_Y$, by the bounded
constructible output of [lem-pur-compact-support-condensed-realization]. Thus $F^c$ preserves the
entire stated subcategory. The inverse geometric isomorphisms give inverses to $P,Q$; they
preserve boundedness and locally finite free strata by relabeling the same strata. The identity
and composition laws in [lem-sf-adic-realization-isomorphism-normalization] and [lem-pur-weil-
semilinear-exchange-reindexing] identify the comparison for the inverse reindexing with the square
for $P^{-1},Q^{-1}$. The comparison transported using $\alpha$ in Step 12 is precisely $a^c$
restricted to these ordinary constructible categories. Uses: lem-sf-adic-qbar-constructible-
realization, lem-pur-compact-support-condensed-realization, lem-sf-adic-realization-isomorphism-
normalization, lem-pur-weil-semilinear-exchange-reindexing.

14. At $\Omega$, $D^b_{ctf}=D^b_c$ by the field-stage definition in [def-weil-finite-tor-
amplitude]. Hence [lem-sf-constructible-exceptional-adjunction-coefficients] applies to the entire
common-stage categories used by $\rho$. Choose quasi-inverses $s_T$ to $\rho_T$. Full
faithfulness, $\alpha$ and that adjunction give the natural Hom bijections
$$\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C_Y}(F^cM,L) \cong\operatorname{Hom}(F_fs_XM,s_YL)
\cong\operatorname{Hom}(s_XM,G_fs_YL) \cong\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C_X}(M,\rho_XG_fs_YL).$$
Apply [lem-weil-pbc-adjunction-of-natural-bijection]: the images of identity arrows define
$\eta,\epsilon$ with both triangle identities. This is the stipulated transported adjunction with
$F=F^c|_{\mathcal C_X}$ and $G=\rho_XG_fs_Y$. Its functors preserve bounded constructible objects
by that coefficient-adjunction statement and the realization equivalences. Only these
constructible Hom sets were used. Uses: def-weil-finite-tor-amplitude, lem-sf-constructible-
exceptional-adjunction-coefficients, lem-sf-adic-qbar-constructible-realization, lem-weil-pbc-
adjunction-of-natural-bijection.

15. For $a^{-1}:FP\xrightarrow{\sim}QF$, [def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate] gives $$b_L:PG
L\xrightarrow{\eta_{PGL}}GFPG L\xrightarrow{G(a^{-1}_{GL})}GQFG
L\xrightarrow{GQ(\epsilon_L)}GQL.$$ Apply [lem-weil-mate-iso-along-equivalences] with $L=L'=F$,
$R=R'=G$, source-side functor $P$, target-side functor $Q$, and square $a^{-1}$. Step 13 makes $P$
an equivalence and $Q$ fully faithful on these exact ordinary constructible categories, so $b$ is
invertible. This checks the side-functor hypotheses required for invertibility of a mate. Uses:
def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate, lem-weil-mate-iso-along-equivalences.

16. Apply [lem-amb-mate-unit-counit] to the same two adjunctions and the square $a^{-1}$. Its
counit identity reads $$\epsilon_{QL}F(b_L)=Q(\epsilon_L)a^{-1}_{GL},$$ with both sides $FPGL\to
QL$. The counit is the one transported in Step 14, because that is the adjunction used when taking
the mate. Uses: lem-amb-mate-unit-counit.

17. Let $L^{\mathrm W}\in\mathcal W(Y_0)$ and $L=U_YL^{\mathrm W}$. Represent it by a complex of
fixed pairs from [def-sf-native-weil-derived-framework]. Its termwise structure maps commute with
the differential, giving an invertible chain map $Q(U_YL^\bullet)\to U_YL^\bullet$. Exact termwise
forgetting descends to ordinary derived categories by [lem-sf-weil-frobenius-action-resolutions],
so this is the specified $\theta:QL\simeq L$, natural under quasi-isomorphisms. Put $N=GL$ and
$v=G(\theta)b_L:PN\simeq N$. Its factors are invertible. Counit naturality for $\theta:QL\to L$
and Step 16 give $$\begin{aligned} \epsilon_LF(v)a_N &=\epsilon_LFG(\theta)F(b_L)a_{GL}\\
&=\theta\epsilon_{QL}F(b_L)a_{GL}\\ &=\theta Q(\epsilon_L)a^{-1}_{GL}a_{GL} =\theta Q(\epsilon_L).
\end{aligned}$$ Both sides are $QFGL\to L$. This uses a structure map on the underlying derived
object of an actual complex of fixed pairs, without identifying its derived category with fixed
pairs in an ordinary derived category. Uses: def-sf-native-weil-derived-framework, lem-sf-weil-
frobenius-action-resolutions.
-/
theorem scalar_frobenius_counit
    (P : ConstructibleExceptionalCoefficients.CoefficientConstruction l
      (ULift.{u} (PadicAlgCl l)) D hl (f := FrobeniusExchange.fiberMap D f₀))
    [G : LocallyFiniteAdicCompactSupport.NativeGeometry.{u} l] :
    ∃ A : CounitData l D R f₀ c₀ S.leftSystem.functor S.rightSystem.functor O.adjunction G,
      Nonempty (ScalarNormalization l D hl R f₀ c₀ S O P A) ∧
      Nonempty (FrobeniusNormalization l D hl R f₀ c₀ S O G A) := by
  obtain ⟨A⟩ := native_pinned_counit_data_exists l D hl R f₀ c₀ S O P
  exact ⟨A.data, ⟨A.scalar⟩, ⟨A.frobenius⟩⟩

end Geometry
end WeilConjectures.NativeWeilScalar
